ent;"></div></figure>
<p>Since one of our operations is <code>left =3D mid</code>, we get stuck i=
n an infinite loop where <code>left</code> and <code>mid</code> are continu=
ously set to each other=E2=80=99s positions, which means progress cannot be=
 made. The reason this wasn=E2=80=99t a problem during lower-bound binary s=
earch was that we never had <code>left =3D mid</code> as an operation in th=
e logic. One way to avoid this issue is by <strong>biasing the midpoint to =
the right</strong>.</p>
<p>When the midpoint is biased to the right, we avoid an infinite loop duri=
ng upper-bound binary search.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>We no longer need to worry about the left pointer since <code>mid</code>=
 is now being positioned at the right pointer when the search space has two=
 elements.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>We won=E2=80=99t encounter any infinite loops with the right pointer bec=
ause it never gets set to the midpoint=E2=80=99s position, as we use <code>=
right =3D mid - 1</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>A right bias of the midpoint can be achieved using <code>mid =3D (left +=
 right) // 2 </code><strong><code>+ 1</code></strong>:</p>
